1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,380
A scheduled job on the build server runs
git fsck, Git's own checker, and mails the

2
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:14,300
output. This morning the mail has one line
in it: "dangling commit", followed by an

3
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:19,101
ID. A colleague replies to the thread: the
repository is damaged, somebody should

4
00:00:19,141 --> 00:00:19,931
run a cleanup.

5
00:00:20,271 --> 00:00:25,459
Your CTO asks you two things. Is data at
risk? And should somebody run a cleanup?

6
00:00:25,799 --> 00:00:30,050
The answer, which you'll be able to defend
at the end of this video: "dangling"

7
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:33,688
describes where git fsck started
looking, not what will be deleted.

8
00:00:33,728 --> 00:00:37,327
And the cleanup is the risky part.
Keep that one-line mail in mind.

9
00:00:37,367 --> 00:00:38,087
It comes back.

10
00:00:39,927 --> 00:00:44,281
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:44,321 --> 00:00:48,602
This video has three parts and
three replays from labs/ch03.

12
00:00:48,642 --> 00:00:54,580
First, reachability: which objects Git can
arrive at, from where, and what git fsck

13
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:55,660
calls the rest.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:04,112
An object is one stored unit, such
as a commit or a file's content.

15
00:01:04,152 --> 00:01:14,089
Second, integrity: an object ID is a
checksum, and you'll see where Git recomputes

16
00:01:14,129 --> 00:01:23,823
it and where it doesn't. Third, a rule
that entered git fsck in Git 2.53.0 about

17
00:01:23,863 --> 00:01:28,081
reflog entries dated in the future.

18
00:01:28,421 --> 00:01:35,269
A reflog is the local journal of the values
a ref, a name such as a branch, has had.

19
00:01:35,309 --> 00:01:41,008
That rule is the reason the lab clock
of this course sits in the past.

20
00:01:41,348 --> 00:01:47,267
git fsck is 🟢 SAFE: it changes nothing. One
command near the end is 🔴 DANGEROUS, git

21
00:01:47,307 --> 00:01:52,161
gc --prune=now, and I'll answer the
five questions before running it.

22
00:01:54,001 --> 00:01:55,414
After this video you can:

23
00:01:55,754 --> 00:01:57,917
Define reachable as Git's maintenance
uses it and list the roots.

24
00:01:58,257 --> 00:02:02,418
Tell a dangling object from an
unreachable one in git fsck output.

25
00:02:02,758 --> 00:02:05,734
Decide whether a dangling commit
line means anything is at risk.

26
00:02:06,074 --> 00:02:10,608
Explain why a corrupt object can pass
local commands and fail a clone.

27
00:02:10,948 --> 00:02:18,240
Explain the git fsck rule about future-dated
reflog entries and the Git version it

28
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:19,134
came with.

29
00:02:20,974 --> 00:02:26,921
Why this matters: garbage collection, Git's
clean-up, deletes objects, and recovery

30
00:02:26,961 --> 00:02:30,671
finds them. Both are defined
by one word, reachable.

31
00:02:30,711 --> 00:02:36,658
If you can say precisely what is reachable,
you know what is safe and for how long.

32
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:41,370
In one sentence: an object is reachable
if you can arrive at it from a starting

33
00:02:41,410 --> 00:02:46,006
point by following the IDs stored inside
objects. git fsck verifies that everything

34
00:02:46,046 --> 00:02:49,859
reachable is present and well-formed,
and lists what nothing reaches.

35
00:02:50,199 --> 00:02:54,140
Now precisely, in the glossary's words: one
object is reachable from another "if we

36
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:58,122
can reach the one from the other by a chain
that follows tags to whatever they tag,

37
00:02:58,162 --> 00:03:01,767
commits to their parents or trees, and
trees to the trees or blobs that they

38
00:03:01,807 --> 00:03:02,199
contain".

39
00:03:02,539 --> 00:03:07,154
Two more terms. An unreachable object
is one that no starting point reaches.

40
00:03:07,194 --> 00:03:12,176
A dangling object is an unreachable object
that no other unreachable object refers

41
00:03:12,216 --> 00:03:13,829
to: the tip of a lost line.

42
00:03:14,169 --> 00:03:19,348
The starting points. The manual of git fsck
lists them as "the index file, all SHA-1

43
00:03:19,388 --> 00:03:24,194
references in the refs namespace, and
all reflogs", and HEAD is added to that.

44
00:03:24,234 --> 00:03:29,288
Four roots, then: the index, which is the
staging area, all refs, all reflogs, and

45
00:03:29,328 --> 00:03:31,338
HEAD, the name for where you are.

46
00:03:31,678 --> 00:03:37,107
Garbage collection keeps the same set alive,
and deletes an unreachable object only

47
00:03:37,147 --> 00:03:41,786
after a grace period: two weeks by
default, the setting gc.pruneExpire.

48
00:03:42,126 --> 00:03:47,624
Inside .git: git fsck changes nothing.
git gc removes unreachable objects from

49
00:03:47,664 --> 00:03:53,303
objects/ once they're past the grace
period, and git reflog expire removes lines

50
00:03:53,343 --> 00:03:54,084
from logs/.

51
00:03:54,424 --> 00:04:00,186
The reflog is the root people forget. Reflog
entries expire: 90 days by default, and

52
00:04:00,226 --> 00:04:05,090
30 days when the commit is no longer
reachable from the tip of the ref.

53
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:10,753
The lab configuration switches both off.
And a deleted branch loses its own reflog

54
00:04:10,793 --> 00:04:16,486
at once. So after you delete a branch, the
entry that still protects its commits is

55
00:04:16,526 --> 00:04:18,006
in the reflog of HEAD.

56
00:04:18,346 --> 00:04:22,561
Now the second topic. An object ID is a
checksum, and Git doesn't recompute it on

57
00:04:22,601 --> 00:04:26,606
every read. A file under objects/ that
is a well-formed object with the wrong

58
00:04:26,646 --> 00:04:28,025
content is served as it is.

59
00:04:28,365 --> 00:04:33,575
Two operations do recompute IDs from
content. git fsck hashes what it finds and

60
00:04:33,615 --> 00:04:38,825
compares the result with the name. And a
transfer through Git's transport sends

61
00:04:38,865 --> 00:04:44,008
object content without IDs. The receiving
side computes every ID itself, so an

62
00:04:44,048 --> 00:04:48,195
object can't arrive under a name
that its content doesn't have.

63
00:04:48,235 --> 00:04:53,577
A clone from a local path skips the transport
and copies or hard-links the files.

64
00:04:53,617 --> 00:04:56,435
The option --no-local
forces the transport.

65
00:04:56,775 --> 00:04:59,623
When should you not act? When
git fsck prints dangling.

66
00:04:59,663 --> 00:05:03,719
That line is information. The lines that
are an incident begin with missing or

67
00:05:03,759 --> 00:05:04,086
error:.

68
00:05:05,926 --> 00:05:09,037
The textbook's analogy is a
library with a catalogue.

69
00:05:09,077 --> 00:05:12,961
A book is findable if a card names
it or a findable book cites it.

70
00:05:13,001 --> 00:05:17,955
A book that nothing names is still on its
shelf, complete, until the next clear-out.

71
00:05:18,295 --> 00:05:23,449
The analogy breaks at direction. In Git,
citations only run from newer to older: a

72
00:05:23,489 --> 00:05:26,300
commit names its parents,
never its children.

73
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:31,535
So losing the card for the newest commit
of a line makes the whole line unfindable

74
00:05:31,575 --> 00:05:36,589
at once. On screen, the branch from today's
demo is deleted, and no branch names its

75
00:05:36,629 --> 00:05:37,552
commit any more.

76
00:05:37,892 --> 00:05:47,627
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. Draw
three commits in a line, with a branch

77
00:05:47,667 --> 00:05:57,644
name on the newest. Erase the name. Which
one commit must you find to get all three

78
00:05:57,684 --> 00:05:59,575
back? I'll wait.

79
00:06:04,415 --> 00:06:09,441
The newest. Carry two consequences from the
model. One: "unfindable" is not "gone".

80
00:06:09,481 --> 00:06:14,202
The objects stay on the shelf until a
clear-out that is weeks away by default.

81
00:06:14,242 --> 00:06:19,085
Two: find the newest commit of a lost
line, the dangling one, and you have found

82
00:06:19,125 --> 00:06:20,367
everything behind it.

83
00:06:22,207 --> 00:06:27,423
First group: the branch main points at
commit 0c2cf43, and from there you reach its

84
00:06:27,463 --> 00:06:32,489
trees, its blobs and its parents. Tags,
the index and HEAD are roots of the same

85
00:06:32,529 --> 00:06:32,806
kind.

86
00:06:33,146 --> 00:06:40,829
Second group: commit 728ac29, its
tree 73c1ade and its blob 35376fb.

87
00:06:40,869 --> 00:06:48,893
No branch names this commit any more.
One reflog entry, HEAD@{1}, does.

88
00:06:48,933 --> 00:06:55,480
These three objects are reachable
through the reflog only.

89
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:03,999
One reflog entry stands between a deleted
branch and the garbage collector.

90
00:07:04,339 --> 00:07:09,458
Third group: blob e6773e7. Nothing
points at it. It's dangling.

91
00:07:13,798 --> 00:07:18,037
A healthy repository: no output, exit
status 0. Silence is the good answer.

92
00:07:18,377 --> 00:07:20,628
Stage a file, then change your mind.

93
00:07:20,968 --> 00:07:26,740
A quick quiz. The file was staged and
unstaged, and git status shows it as

94
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:33,259
untracked. What does git fsck print now:
nothing, an error, or a line about a blob?

95
00:07:33,299 --> 00:07:34,201
Your answer?

96
00:07:39,041 --> 00:07:42,940
A dangling blob. Unstaging removed an
index entry, not the blob that git add had

97
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:46,436
written. Anyone with access to this
directory can still read the token.

98
00:07:46,476 --> 00:07:49,291
The same fact saves you on the
day you lose a staged file.

99
00:07:49,631 --> 00:07:52,519
Now a commit on a branch, and
then the branch is deleted.

100
00:07:52,559 --> 00:07:56,835
Deleting a branch with -D was introduced
with its risk label in the branches videos.

101
00:07:56,875 --> 00:08:00,328
Here it runs in a sandbox, and the
point is what remains afterwards.

102
00:08:00,668 --> 00:08:06,105
With the reflogs excluded as starting points:
how many objects are unreachable, and

103
00:08:06,145 --> 00:08:09,866
how many of them are dangling?
Say both numbers out loud.

104
00:08:09,906 --> 00:08:10,526
I'll wait.

105
00:08:15,366 --> 00:08:20,030
Four are unreachable: the commit, its
tree, its new blob, and the blob from the

106
00:08:20,070 --> 00:08:24,615
previous step. Two are dangling. The
tree 73c1ade and the blob 35376fb aren't

107
00:08:24,655 --> 00:08:27,950
dangling, because the unreachable
commit refers to them.

108
00:08:27,990 --> 00:08:31,344
If you said four and four,
that's the usual first answer.

109
00:08:31,684 --> 00:08:35,377
The reflog of HEAD still records commit
728ac29. With reflog entries as starting

110
00:08:35,417 --> 00:08:37,757
points again, plain git
fsck reports only the blob.

111
00:08:37,797 --> 00:08:39,390
The commit was never at risk today.

112
00:08:42,230 --> 00:08:46,579
The script then overwrites that loose object
file with a valid zlib stream: the same

113
00:08:46,619 --> 00:08:50,184
header, the same length, other
content. This substitution is crafted.

114
00:08:50,224 --> 00:08:54,311
Random damage, such as a flipped bit,
normally breaks the zlib stream and fails

115
00:08:54,351 --> 00:08:55,618
loudly on the first read.

116
00:08:55,958 --> 00:09:00,340
The content under that ID is now
wrong. Will git cat-file complain?

117
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:03,839
Will git status? Yes or no
for each. Say it out loud.

118
00:09:08,679 --> 00:09:15,038
Neither does. Ordinary commands read the
object by its name and don't recompute the

119
00:09:15,078 --> 00:09:15,423
hash.

120
00:09:15,763 --> 00:09:20,331
git fsck reports hash-path mismatch
and missing blob, with exit status 3.

121
00:09:20,371 --> 00:09:24,939
The clone through the transport fails:
"did not receive expected object".

122
00:09:24,979 --> 00:09:29,168
The clone by file copy succeeds and
carries the bad object with it.

123
00:09:29,208 --> 00:09:34,217
That is the fourth objective: a corrupt
object passes local commands and fails a

124
00:09:34,257 --> 00:09:37,563
clone, because only the
transfer recomputes every ID.

125
00:09:37,903 --> 00:09:38,263
The repair.

126
00:09:38,208 --> 00:09:43,319
The working tree still holds the true
content, and content alone determines the ID.

127
00:09:43,359 --> 00:09:48,346
Git won't rewrite an object it believes
it has, so the bad file is removed first.

128
00:09:48,386 --> 00:09:52,939
Deleting a file under objects/ by hand
is damage in every other situation.

129
00:09:52,979 --> 00:09:57,035
Here it's the documented repair,
for one file that git fsck named.

130
00:09:57,075 --> 00:09:59,765
git hash-object -w is
🟢: it adds one object.

131
00:10:00,105 --> 00:10:06,675
True content in, true ID
out: e2238784, as before.

132
00:10:09,515 --> 00:10:15,743
A small script makes two commits and a reset
that drops the second one, all recorded

133
00:10:15,783 --> 00:10:20,594
at a given date. It runs twice:
once dated 2001, once dated 2099.

134
00:10:20,934 --> 00:10:24,369
The two repositories have the same shape.
Will git fsck say the same thing about

135
00:10:24,409 --> 00:10:25,498
both, or not? Your answer?

136
00:10:30,338 --> 00:10:36,040
It does not. In the repository dated
2099, the dropped commit is reported as

137
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:38,986
dangling, although the reflog lists it.

138
00:10:39,326 --> 00:10:42,526
The version note: older Git treated
every reflog entry as a starting point.

139
00:10:42,866 --> 00:10:47,843
Since Git 2.53.0, entries dated after the
moment fsck starts are skipped, and fsck

140
00:10:47,883 --> 00:10:52,553
reads the system clock for this. The
textbook verified it locally: Git 2.55.0

141
00:10:52,593 --> 00:10:57,325
applies the rule, Apple's Git 2.50.1 does
not. So the two Gits on your Mac can

142
00:10:57,365 --> 00:10:59,160
disagree about one repository.

143
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:03,686
Last step, the 🔴 command: git gc
--prune=now. The five answers.

144
00:11:03,726 --> 00:11:09,120
What it changes: it packs the repository
and deletes unreachable objects at once,

145
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:14,621
with no grace period. What it can destroy:
every unreachable object, which is what

146
00:11:14,661 --> 00:11:19,854
recovery depends on. How to preview: git
fsck --unreachable, and git prune -n.

147
00:11:20,194 --> 00:11:23,728
How to recover: from another
clone or a backup only.

148
00:11:23,768 --> 00:11:29,295
When it's appropriate: after a deliberate
history rewrite, in a repository you've

149
00:11:29,335 --> 00:11:34,931
copied first, never as routine maintenance.
Here it runs on a throwaway sandbox to

150
00:11:34,971 --> 00:11:40,360
prove one point. Twig looks worried, and
with a red label on screen, fairly so.

151
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:47,659
Even with no grace period, the commit stays:
garbage collection does not apply the

152
00:11:47,699 --> 00:11:54,829
clock rule. And fsck still calls it dangling.
"Dangling" is a statement about fsck's

153
00:11:54,869 --> 00:11:57,475
starting points, not a verdict.

154
00:11:59,315 --> 00:12:00,963
Five mistakes to watch for.

155
00:12:01,303 --> 00:12:07,019
Running a prune because git fsck printed
dangling. Root cause: dangling means no

156
00:12:07,059 --> 00:12:13,064
fsck starting point reaches the object; the
prune is what destroys recoverable work.

157
00:12:13,404 --> 00:12:20,121
Counting on the branch's reflog
after deleting the branch.

158
00:12:20,161 --> 00:12:29,442
Root cause: a deleted branch loses its
own reflog at once; the reflog of HEAD is

159
00:12:29,482 --> 00:12:32,704
what still holds the commit.

160
00:12:33,044 --> 00:12:36,825
Assuming that unstaging a
file removes its content.

161
00:12:36,865 --> 00:12:42,969
Root cause: git add wrote a blob, and
removing the index entry does not remove the

162
00:12:43,009 --> 00:12:43,493
object.

163
00:12:43,833 --> 00:12:52,815
Trusting a local-path clone as an
integrity check. Root cause: it copies or

164
00:12:52,855 --> 00:13:02,197
hard-links object files and verifies
nothing; --no-local forces the transport.

165
00:13:02,537 --> 00:13:08,956
Two Git versions disagreeing about a
repository. Root cause: the future-dated reflog

166
00:13:08,996 --> 00:13:12,108
rule exists only in Git 2.53.0 and later.

167
00:13:13,948 --> 00:13:18,424
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform team
keeps a mirror of its training-code

168
00:13:18,464 --> 00:13:21,989
repository on the build server
and a backup on another host.

169
00:13:22,029 --> 00:13:26,980
They run git fsck on a schedule against
both, and their runbook has two lines: treat

170
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:31,198
missing and error: lines as an incident,
and dangling lines as routine.

171
00:13:31,538 --> 00:13:35,668
They take the backup through Git's
transport, git clone --mirror from a URL or

172
00:13:35,708 --> 00:13:39,998
--no-local from a path, when they want
every hash recomputed on the way, and as a

173
00:13:40,038 --> 00:13:42,564
file copy when they also
need reflogs and hooks.

174
00:13:42,904 --> 00:13:47,841
And the same reachability rule is the
precise form of an uncomfortable fact about

175
00:13:47,881 --> 00:13:53,002
leaked secrets: removing a commit from every
branch leaves it in the object database

176
00:13:53,042 --> 00:13:54,661
of every clone that has it.

177
00:13:56,501 --> 00:14:01,007
Your turn. Do Exercise 16.7,
"Which git fsck lines matter", in

178
00:14:01,047 --> 00:14:07,019
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md. Before you
look anything up, sort each line of the

179
00:14:07,059 --> 00:14:13,105
output into "information" or "incident" and
write down the check you would make for

180
00:14:13,145 --> 00:14:13,471
each.

181
00:14:13,811 --> 00:14:19,180
The challenge is Exercise 16.9, "Removed
from history, and still there", in the same

182
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:19,502
file.

183
00:14:21,342 --> 00:14:24,095
Question 78 of the CTO question bank:

184
00:14:24,435 --> 00:14:28,790
A strong answer defines dangling against
the starting points of git fsck before it

185
00:14:28,830 --> 00:14:33,131
does anything else. It then names the
checks in order: what the reflog says, what

186
00:14:33,171 --> 00:14:37,580
the clock and the Git version could be doing
to the result, what garbage collection

187
00:14:37,620 --> 00:14:41,814
would and would not delete and when. It
finishes with the action that keeps the

188
00:14:41,854 --> 00:14:45,994
commit, and with what must not be run.
Avoid answering with a cleanup command.

189
00:14:47,834 --> 00:14:52,436
Let's land this. That one-line mail was
information, and the cleanup was the risk.

190
00:14:52,776 --> 00:15:11,492
You should now be able to say:

191
00:15:11,832 --> 00:15:15,318
Reachable means arrived at from the
index, a ref, a reflog entry or HEAD by

192
00:15:15,358 --> 00:15:16,682
following IDs inside objects.

193
00:15:17,022 --> 00:15:21,137
Unreachable objects are kept for a grace
period; dangling ones are the tips of

194
00:15:21,177 --> 00:15:22,096
unreachable lines.

195
00:15:22,436 --> 00:15:25,025
A dangling line is information; missing
and error: lines are an incident.

196
00:15:25,365 --> 00:15:30,241
Ordinary commands do not rehash what they
read; git fsck and Git's transport do.

197
00:15:30,581 --> 00:15:35,947
Since Git 2.53.0, git fsck skips reflog
entries dated in the future; git gc does not

198
00:15:35,987 --> 00:15:36,977
apply that rule.

199
00:15:38,817 --> 00:15:50,724
Read section 3.8 of Chapter 3. Do Exercise
16.5, "What git gc does with unreachable

200
00:15:50,764 --> 00:15:57,057
objects", in
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

201
00:15:57,397 --> 00:16:01,190
Today you learned to read a dangling line
calmly. Do the exercise before the next

202
00:16:01,230 --> 00:16:04,549
video: what makes a repository slow,
and the two commands that tidy it.

203
00:16:04,589 --> 00:16:07,010
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

204
00:16:07,050 --> 00:16:08,146
See you in the next one.
